Subject: [PATCH 23/23] client.fs: load-state is never zeroed, and x86's `go` walked 768 MB
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 13.1 recorded "x86's `go` hangs where amd64's completes" as an UNKNOWN.
It is not a context-switch bug and it is not arch-specific code. It is one
uninitialised structure and a sentinel that could not mean what it was read to
mean.

    struct ( load-state ) ... constant load-state.size
    create load-state load-state.size allot     <- allot does NOT zero

Nothing else initialises it. The six C loaders in libopenbios/*_load.c each fill
in >ls.file-size for their own format; the $load path fills in none of it. And
libopenbios/initprogram.c's init_forth_context() reads that field and treats
ZERO AS "NOBODY WROTE THIS":

    feval("load-state >ls.file-size @");
    size = POP();
    if (size == 0) { feval("load-size"); size = POP(); }
    fword("load-base"); PUSH(size); fword("evaluate");

Measured 2026-08-26, same firmware, same 31-byte .fth, both arches loading it
correctly (load-size 0x1f, first byte 0x5c on both):

    load-state >ls.file-size @      amd64 -> 0           x86 -> 30000000

On amd64 the field happened to be zero, the fallback fired, 31 bytes evaluated,
`go` worked. On x86 it held 0x30000000 of dictionary garbage, so the fallback
did NOT fire and `evaluate` was handed 768 MEGABYTES to interpret as Forth.
That is the hang. amd64 had been passing BY LUCK, on the contents of memory.

THE FIX IS THREE LINES AND ONE PRINTK:

  1. client.fs ERASES load-state at creation, so 0 finally means "nothing was
     loaded" rather than "nobody wrote it down". This covers >ls.entry and
     >ls.file-type too -- they have the same exposure and no reader had any
     way to know.
  2. !load-size writes BOTH records of the one fact. `variable file-size` and
     `load-state >ls.file-size` hold the same number and only the first was
     being set on the $load path, which is the "two records" defect patch 15's
     comment named and worked around rather than fixed. Ordering is safe:
     $load calls !load-size BEFORE init-program, so a C loader that later
     writes its own authoritative value still wins.
  3. A zero size is now a REFUSAL WITH A REASON. `evaluate` of 0 bytes
     succeeds and prints nothing, so a size that never got written looked
     exactly like a payload with nothing to say -- the same silence this lab
     has now chased four times (patches 14, 16, 18, 20).

AFTER, x86 NO LONGER HANGS. It says what happened instead:

    0 > go
    switching to new context:
    Evaluating Forth...
    init-program: nothing to evaluate -- ls.file-size=0 load-size=0
    load-base=4000000 -- no bytes were loaded, or this context cannot see them

WHICH SURFACES A SECOND, SEPARATE x86 DEFECT -- now honest instead of hanging,
and NOT fixed here. At the prompt, x86 reads load-size=1f and
load-base=e066fdd0 (the C shadow arch/x86/openbios.c defines as
phys_to_virt(LOAD_BASE_PHYS)). INSIDE the client context the same two words
read 0 and 4000000 -- and 0x4000000 is the constant in forth/admin/nvram.fs,
a DIFFERENT WORD from the one the prompt resolves. So this is about which
definition is visible from inside the context, not about address translation;
a `constant` would carry the same number through any segment change. Mechanism
beyond that is NOT established and no guess is recorded in its place.
amd64 is unaffected (it defines no shadow -- it does not relocate) and runs the
payload.

So `go` on x86 goes from a silent 768 MB walk to a named refusal, which is the
whole of what this patch claims. Running the payload there is the next item.

All 13 smoke tracks pass on x86, amd64 and ppc.

diff --git a/forth/debugging/client.fs b/forth/debugging/client.fs
--- a/forth/debugging/client.fs
+++ b/forth/debugging/client.fs
@@ -31,12 +31,45 @@ struct ( load-state )
 constant load-state.size
 create load-state load-state.size allot
 
+\ ERASED, and it is load-bearing. `create ... allot` hands back whatever was in
+\ the dictionary space, and NOTHING else initialises this structure: the six C
+\ loaders in libopenbios/*_load.c each fill in >ls.file-size for their own
+\ format, and the $load path below fills in none of it. So every reader that
+\ treats 0 as "nobody wrote this" was asking a question the storage could not
+\ answer.
+\
+\ Measured 2026-08-26, and it is exactly why `go` hung on x86 and not on amd64:
+\
+\     load /ide@1/cdrom@0:\go.fth   then   load-state >ls.file-size @
+\       amd64  ->  0            ( so libopenbios/initprogram.c fell back to
+\                                 load-size, 31 bytes, and it worked )
+\       x86    ->  30000000     ( so it did NOT fall back, and handed
+\                                 `evaluate` 768 MB to interpret as Forth )
+\
+\ Same firmware, same file, same 31 bytes correctly loaded on both. The only
+\ difference was what happened to be lying in the dictionary at that address.
+\ amd64 had been passing BY LUCK.
+load-state load-state.size erase
+
 variable state-valid
 0 state-valid !
 
 variable file-size
 
-: !load-size file-size ! ;
+: !load-size  ( n -- )
+  \ BOTH RECORDS OF THE ONE FACT. `variable file-size` and
+  \ `load-state >ls.file-size` are two places holding the same number, and
+  \ before this only the first was written here while libopenbios reads the
+  \ second -- so a file loaded through $load reported its size to nobody who
+  \ was asking. Writing both at the point the fact is learned is what makes
+  \ the erase above mean something: 0 now says "nothing was loaded", not
+  \ "nothing wrote it down".
+  \
+  \ Ordering is safe: $load calls !load-size BEFORE init-program, so a C
+  \ loader that later fills in its own authoritative value still wins.
+  dup file-size !
+  load-state >ls.file-size !
+;
 
 : load-size file-size @ ;
 
--- a/libopenbios/initprogram.c
+++ b/libopenbios/initprogram.c
@@ -151,6 +151,41 @@
 			size = POP();
 		}
 
+		/* A ZERO HERE IS A REFUSAL, NOT A NO-OP.
+		 *
+		 * `evaluate` of 0 bytes succeeds and prints nothing, so before
+		 * this a size that never got written looked exactly like a
+		 * payload that had nothing to say -- the same silence this
+		 * lab has now chased four times.
+		 *
+		 * The 0-means-unset test above is kept as belt and braces, but
+		 * it is no longer what makes this correct: client.fs now
+		 * ERASES load-state at creation and !load-size writes BOTH
+		 * records, so 0 finally means "nothing was loaded" rather than
+		 * "nobody wrote it down". It did not mean that before, and
+		 * that is precisely how `go` hung on x86: the field held
+		 * 0x30000000 of dictionary garbage, the fallback therefore did
+		 * not fire, and `evaluate` was handed 768 MB to interpret. */
+		if (size == 0) {
+			ucell fs, ls, lb;
+
+			/* Report the three values SEPARATELY. "both 0" cannot
+			 * distinguish "nothing was loaded" from "this context
+			 * cannot see what was loaded", and on x86 those are
+			 * different answers: at the prompt load-size reads
+			 * 0x1f, and here it reads 0. */
+			feval("load-state >ls.file-size @"); fs = POP();
+			feval("load-size");                  ls = POP();
+			feval("load-base");                  lb = POP();
+			printk("init-program: nothing to evaluate --"
+			       " ls.file-size=%lx load-size=%lx load-base=%lx"
+			       " -- no bytes were loaded, or this context"
+			       " cannot see them\n",
+			       (unsigned long)fs, (unsigned long)ls,
+			       (unsigned long)lb);
+			return;
+		}
+
 		fword("load-base");
 		PUSH(size);
 	}
